  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Increasing chances for rain Friday afternoon\nthrough Sunday night. \n\nA sub-tropical ridge now overhead will gradually be pushed\nsoutheast by Friday night. Broad troughing covering the \nnorthern half of the CONUS will shift east and periodically dip \nfarther south into the Southern Appalachians through early next\nweek. A cold front is projected to shift through WV and VA and \nslow over NC Friday into Saturday before stalling. This will be\naccompanied by a string of positive vorticity advection on the \nfront side of the trough. The front will meander in the vicinity\nas a warm front, and along with additional swaths of PVA, will\ntrigger widespread rain chances Friday night through Sunday \nnight. By Monday, a secondary cold front will sweep through, \nthis time accompanied by cooler and much drier air, bringing an \nend to our rain chances. \n\nThe trend to delay the onset of precipitation has held...with\nthe first drops likely falling in SE WV tomorrow late afternoon\ninto the evening, and not pushing into SW VA until Saturday \nmorning and NW NC until Saturday afternoon. Rainfall amounts \nlook to be a half an inch to over an inch for the entire event, \nbut some CAMs show more spotty precipitation with some areas \npossibly missing out or only receiving drizzle or a light \nshower. Could see a few thunderstorms tomorrow, but for the \nweekend with wedging building in, expect just general rain \nshowers, some of which could have moderate rain rates. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A warming trend continues tomorrow, with a cold\nair wedge building in for the weekend. Nice fall weather, a \nlittle on the cool side, for next week. \n\nThrough late Friday, WSW winds will help pump moisture northward\nto the baroclinic zone moving into our area. This along with \nresidual ridging and compressional warming along the boundary \nwill continue to support above normal temperatures through \ntomorrow. We'll range from about +9F degrees above climatology \nfor Bluefield to +13F degrees above for Roanoke and Danville for\ntomorrow's highs. Did lower them a bit based on encroaching \ncloud cover. \n\nA wedge pattern begins to develop Saturday and becomes more \npronounced through Sunday, eventually being eroded by the large\npush of high pressure and cool, dry air behind the secondary \nfront Monday. Temperatures will drop into the 60s and 70s \nareawide Saturday through next Wednesday...much closer to or \neven below normal...perfect sweater weather. Sunny skies will go\nsome way to making it more of a pleasant fall weather stretch.",
